rden on  those with stronger economies many  others however still see the euro as the  best opportunity for the european union  countries to grow even closer  [Music]  well to discuss further 20 years of the  euro let's speak to maria demensis who's  deputy director of the brussels economic  think tank brugal thanks a lot for  joining us on dw business it's great to  have you on so two decades in can we say  whether the euro has been a success or  not  yes of course i think the euro has been  uh has been a a big success actually and  and a way of thinking about this is  imagine how countries small countries  but also big countries would have  weathered  the two big crisis that we've seen in  the past 20 years had they been alone  i think that's a very important thing to  try and contemplate as of a thought  experiment and then you realize that the  scale and the stability that the euro  has brought to the continent different  degrees for different countries has  actually managed to keep uh europe  together and actually weather the crisis  with much less cost than it would have  been otherwise so yes it has been an  outright success as far as i'm concerned  but as you just mentioned the impact of  it has varied between countries have  there been winners and losers from the  last 20 years  i think it's important to appreciate  that not all countries joined uh the  euro indeed the eu for the same reasons  uh to the extent that one can have a  distinction between the north and the  south and i'm very reluctant to make  these types of comparisons because  they're never really accurate but let's  say for the sake of conversation we we  divide the continent in these two blocks  the northern countries are more open  countries so they are the ones who are  looking  for a greater markets so they're real  the prime motivation for the northern  part of europe to join the eu is to  expand their markets both domestically  domestically meaning in europe but  through scale also internationally  globally  and and that of course the euro provided  amply to countries of the north  countries in the south on the other hand  wanted stability they wanted a stable  currency that is going to ensure that  the consumer has got predictability in  terms of purchasing power in their  pockets and they got up with the euro so  yeah to that extent if you sort of think  about the motivation why every country  joined the eu  then all of them had something to uh to  win from  the adjustment to the uh to the euro  hasn't been easy for all countries that  i think is is important countries in the  south found it a lot more difficult to  adapt simply because their economies are  a lot more inflexible  but i think even countries in the south  have found the stability of the euro to  be an option right success it survived  two decades and it's got through various  crises but there is another one on the  way as the eurozone recovers from the  pandemic how well set is the euro to get  through that  i think you'll see that uh european  architecture which is absolutely  essential for a good architecture i mean  for sustaining shocks or sustaining  crisis has been going has been moving in  the right direction compare the reaction  how europe dealt with the financial  crisis uh and at what speed and to the  reaction of the eu in the current crisis  the pandemic crisis in 2020 and at what  speed you'll see that both the unity of  europe as well as the speed of reaction  have greatly improved there is a thing a  sincere realization appreciation of the  fact that a united europe can really  weather these storms much faster there  are very important discussions in 2022  when it comes to architectural issues  i'm thinking mostly of the fiscal pact  that can really help increase the  resilience of the continent and i think  there is enough understanding that  important steps forward need to happen  okay maria de meza's from brugel thank  you very much for joining us on dw  business
 

